<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1946</h1><p class="ref-lede">1946 in world history: 23 events, 15 births, 32 deaths, 1 work, 19 states founded and 30 that came to an end. 710 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a><a href="/years/1943">1943</a><a href="/years/1944">1944</a><a href="/years/1945">1945</a><a href="/years/1946" aria-current="page">1946</a><a href="/years/1947">1947</a><a href="/years/1948">1948</a><a href="/years/1949">1949</a><a href="/years/1950">1950</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/london" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-10">10 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">First UN General Assembly meets in London</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Delegates from 51 nations gathered at Methodist Central Hall, Westminster, for the first session of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a>. The UN later moved its headquarters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-11">11 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Albania proclaimed a People's Republic</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Albania's assembly abolished the monarchy and declared a People's Republic under communist leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a>. His regime became one of the most isolated and repressive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-17">17 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">UN Security Council meets for the first time</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> held its first session at Church House, Westminster. Its five permanent members, each with a veto, were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-1">1 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Trygve Lie becomes the first UN Secretary-General</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The General Assembly appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s foreign minister Trygve Lie as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-general-of-the-united-nations">Secretary-General of the United Nations</a>. He served until 1952, resigning after Soviet opposition over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-14">14 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">ENIAC is unveiled at the University of Pennsylvania</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The US Army publicly revealed ENIAC, the first programmable general-purpose electronic computer. With nearly 18,000 vacuum tubes, it could perform 5,000 additions a second.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-24">24 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Juan Perón elected President of Argentina</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The former labour minister won the presidency with strong support from trade unions. With his wife Eva, he built the populist Peronist movement that still shapes Argentine politics.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-5">5 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Churchill warns of an 'iron curtain' across Europe</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Speaking at Fulton, Missouri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> declared that an iron curtain had descended across the continent from the Baltic to the Adriatic. The speech popularised the phrase and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> divide.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-17">17 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Last French troops leave Syria</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The withdrawal of the final French forces completed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>'s independence after a quarter-century of mandate rule. The day is still marked in Syria as Evacuation Day.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-4">4 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Philippines gains independence from the United States</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> became a sovereign republic after nearly half a century of American rule, as promised by the 1934 Tydings-McDuffie Act. For decades 4 July was celebrated as its Independence Day.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/paris" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-5">5 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Louis Réard unveils the bikini at a Paris swimming pool</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Engineer and designer Louis Réard presented his two-piece swimsuit at the Piscine Molitor, modelled by dancer Micheline Bernardini. He named it after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bikini-atoll">Bikini Atoll</a>, where atomic tests had just begun.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuremberg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-16">16 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Ten Nazi leaders are hanged at Nuremberg</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Ten men sentenced by the International Military Tribunal, including Ribbentrop, Keitel and Streicher, were hanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> prison's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a>. Göring had taken poison the night before.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-world-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-31">31 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Truman proclaims the end of hostilities in the Second World War</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">President Truman formally proclaimed the cessation of hostilities, more than a year after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender. The legal state of war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Japan ended later, with peace treaties.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/monnet-plan" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monnet-plan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/monnet-plan">Monnet Plan</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Monnet Plan was France's first plan for reconstruction and modernisation, drawn up by Jean Monnet's planning commission and adopted in 1946.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Revolution</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/east-sumatra-revolution">East Sumatra revolution</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The East Sumatra revolution was an uprising in March 1946 in which armed youth groups and leftist militias attacked the Malay and Simalungun aristocracies of eastern Sumatra.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/washington-agreement-1946">Washington Agreement (1946)</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Washington Agreement of May 1946 settled Allied claims against Switzerland arising from its wartime dealings with Nazi Germany.</p></div></li></ol></section>
